./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:73:Overly conservative. Earlier versions of the same-path multi-threshold lift subtracted an \(O(m)\) cap for learner rewards from intermediate threshold-round posts. Iteration 8 shows this loss cancels in the finite block when thresholds are increasing and regret uses comparator suprema: at horizon \(T_j=M+N+j\), the comparator quote \(a\uparrow c_j\) also has \(j-1\) intermediate threshold-round earning opportunities, exactly matching the crude \((j-1)c_j\) cap on the learner's intermediate rewards. The correct finite bound is \(N\kappa(C)-M\), not \(N\kappa(C)-M-O(m)\). The denominator in diagonal concatenations still requires \(m_i=o(N_i)\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:77:Invalid as stated. The identity that a zero-block quote \(A_s\) has traded by \(T_j\) iff \(A_s<c_j\) uses \(0<c_1<\cdots<c_m\) in path order. If the thresholds are unsorted, the effective threshold by \(T_j\) is \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), and the \(\kappa(C)\) formula for the displayed order no longer follows directly.
