./working_notes/counterexamples.md:163:if the learner is told the evaluation horizon \(T_j=M+N+j\), it can tailor its zero-block quotes to \(c_j\). The common zero-block action law across \(j\), which is the input to the \(\kappa(C)\) averaging argument, is then absent. This is the same obstruction as the horizon/process-aware oracle collapse: CL-026 and CL-027 apply only to one infinite horizon-oblivious policy.
./iterations/iter_008/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:- **Missing assumption:** the policy must be genuinely horizon-oblivious. The zero-block actions \(A_1,\ldots,A_N\) must be the same random variables for all evaluations at \(T_j=M+N+j\). If the learner is given \(T_j\), the \(\kappa(C)\) averaging argument collapses.
