./iterations/iter_007/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
