./logs/iter_008_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Bobby Kleinberg, Renato Paes Leme, Jon Schneider, Yifeng Teng","relevance":"Useful analogy for the prefix-compatibility program: U-calibration is a maximal regret metric over all downstream bounded scoring rules, similar in spirit to one action sequence serving many candidate horizons or continuations.","title":"U-Calibration: Forecasting for an Unknown Agent","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/kleinberg23a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 195, 2023"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Yifan Wu","relevance":"Decision-theoretic calibration loss is a probability-weighted worst-case payoff-loss notion; it may supply terminology and LP analogies for replacing raw delay tails by decision-relevant information loss.","title":"Calibration Error for Decision Making","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13503","venue_year":"FOCS 2024 / arXiv:2404.13503"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan","relevance":"Gives minimax results for simultaneous regret over loss families; relevant as an analogy for simultaneous-prefix and vector-deficit formulations, not as direct support for delayed market making.","title":"Optimal Multiclass U-Calibration Error and Beyond","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=7aFRgCC8Q7","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Konstantina Bairaktari, Lunjia Hu, Huy L. Nguyen, Jonathan Ullman","relevance":"Recent actionable calibration work emphasizes measures that directly upper-bound downstream swap regret; useful terminology for an actionable delay-tail or information-loss statistic.","title":"Testable and Actionable Calibration for Full Swap Regret","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17749","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The cancellation can fail if the policy model leaks the evaluation horizon, if endpoint timing is misstated, or if strict-crossing limits are handled with a fixed eta that scales with N or m.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing cleanup","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Ask an explorer to turn the iteration-8 same-path multi-threshold argument into a formal finite lemma. For path old-prefix length M, then 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, verify for each horizon T_j=M+N+j that the comparator supremum using a quote approaching c_j from below is at least (N+j-1)c_j, while learner rewards from intermediate threshold-round actions before T_j are at most (j-1)c_j. This should yield R_Tj >= N c_j - sum_s A_s 1{A_s<c_j} - M.","title":"Audit The No-Loss Same-Path Lift","why_promising":"If correct, this promotes CL-026 in a stronger form with no O(m) finite-block loss and is the cleanest immediate extension of the accepted two-threshold anytime obstruction."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The proof must track the denominator T_j=M_i+N_i+j and require m_i=o(N_i). It also must be explicit that the learner is process-aware but horizon-oblivious; a horizon-aware oracle avoids the lower bound.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","asymptotic bookkeeping","model specification"],"summary":"Use the finite same-path lemma to build one deterministic known path with blocks 0^{N_i},C_i, where C_i is the m_i-point equal-revenue threshold menu, m_i -> infinity, and M_i+m_i=o(N_i). Prove that every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e.","title":"Diagonal Same-Path 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"It would give the exact same-path analogue of the accepted universal terminal-block 1/e lift and sharply separates anytime learning from horizon-aware oracle policies."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A local randomized primal strategy may not compose into a global anytime upper bound. Strict crossing also requires quotes just below thresholds or a limiting argument.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","tightness check","constructive upper bound"],"summary":"Solve the primal local zero-block game for finite C: minimize over action laws mu the maximum over c in C of c - E_mu[a 1{a<c}]. For equal-revenue thresholds, verify the solver's claim that the uniform law over quotes just below c_i has constant deficit r^m, matching the dual kappa(C). Then characterize the primal optimizer for arbitrary C.","title":"Primal Kappa Geometry","why_promising":"This would certify tightness of the finite multi-threshold lower bound and may reveal whether the block lower bounds are also local upper bounds for known-path anytime design."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The condition may become tautological if defined as the final regret itself. Overlapping future maxima across blocks may prevent additive decomposition into local kappas.","suggested_lenses":["characterization attempt","counterexample search","offline optimization"],"summary":"Formulate the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix problem: choose one action sequence so that V_T^* - reward_T is small for all T. Test candidate compatibility quantities on 0^N,c_1,...,c_m blocks, sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, and superexponential uniform spikes.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Compatibility","why_promising":"This is the most plausible quantifier regime for a real characterization: oracle collapse no longer applies, but raw beta failure is still not necessary."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A lower-bound modulus may not imply sufficiency. Rare events, suffix compensation, and process-aware coding can make any unweighted or purely local condition misleading.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","stress testing","literature mapping"],"summary":"Develop a probability-weighted information-loss modulus based on common-prefix events: p_u times a local kappa_I(U), minus an outside-surplus cap B_u. Compare it with U-calibration and Calibration Decision Loss as decision-relevant replacements for raw error metrics. Test it against CE-001, CE-003, CE-005, and same-path blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It directly targets the main obstruction in the notes: long delayed mass matters only when it is payoff-relevant, unpredictable, frequent enough, and not compensated later."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The all-horizons expected-regret calculation may be delicate. If the hard branch is not revealed early enough, even a process-aware learner may suffer p_i N_i regret; if it is too revealed, the essential-sup hardness may become artificial but still useful as a stress test.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Construct a rare-branch process where, with probability p_i, a code reveals entry into a long equal-revenue multi-threshold block with kappa close to 1/e, but p_i N_i is chosen to be negligible relative to relevant horizons. Compute beta, gamma, conditional kappa, Delta_T, and expected regret for law-aware policies.","title":"Rare-Branch Multi-Threshold Stress Test","why_promising":"This would sharpen CE-006 and show that essential-sup or conditional hardness must be probability-weighted before it can support a necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Most arbitrary-delay results are bandit or OCO rather than public-price full-information market making. The explorer must explicitly map reward-vector observability and skipped payoff loss.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","model mapping","literature audit"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping or outstanding-observation delayed-learning algorithms to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for a bound of the form delayed-learning term plus payoff-weighted skipped-fill loss plus T/K, without knowing a tail function beta or gamma in advance.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It strengthens the settled sufficiency side and aligns the theorem with modern delayed-feedback literature, where missing information and skipped observations are often more relevant than raw elapsed delay."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_006_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2046:iterations/iter_006/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli","relevance":"Adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful terminology and contrast for delayed-fill feedback.","title":"Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"New delayed OCO model with tracking capacity and delayed-weighted reductions; relevant to adaptive truncation/skipping and outstanding-information bounds.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang","relevance":"Develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions, a possible framework for prefix-compatibility or simultaneous-horizon guarantees.","title":"Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider","relevance":"Recent simultaneous-regret work; useful analogy for one sequence of predictions/actions satisfying many downstream losses or horizons.","title":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jason D. Hartline","relevance":"Contains equal-revenue distribution and monopoly-pricing/revenue-curve background that may directly match the multi-threshold block LP.","title":"Mechanism Design and Approximation, Chapter 4: Bayesian Approximation","url":"https://jasonhartline.com/MDnA/MDnA-ch4.pdf","venue_year":"online textbook chapter"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The 1/e constant may fail under finite-support approximation, strict crossing, or transfer to concatenated paths; multi-horizon same-path versions must control intermediate quote rewards when the number of thresholds grows.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","lower-bound constants","mechanism-design analogy","strict-threshold cleanup"],"summary":"Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).","title":"Multi-Threshold Block Constant","why_promising":"This is a concrete, separately checkable extension of the accepted 1/4 lemma and may strengthen the lower-bound constant while connecting kappa to known posted-price geometry."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The prefix-local comparator v_u must be a valid lower bound on the global fixed-action comparator; same-prefix laws can fail for stochastic candidates; rare events and suffix compensation can otherwise create false positives.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's kappa transfer into a precise lemma. Work on finite grids and finite candidate set U first: condition on a common-prefix event E, assume the learner's prefix action marginals are identical for all u in U, define prefix payoffs phi_{u,t}, and include an explicit outside-surplus cap B_u plus probability weight Pr(E). Prove max_u regret_u >= |I| kappa_I(U)-B, or identify the missing hypotheses.","title":"Kappa Transfer Lemma","why_promising":"It would convert the current prefix-compatibility heuristic into an accepted reusable lower-bound tool, and every assumption can be criticized directly."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A full characterization may become tautological or computational rather than structural; the best fixed comparator couples all times through one quote, while the learner can vary quotes by time.","suggested_lenses":["offline characterization","finite examples","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Study the process-aware but horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix design problem. Given a known path P_1,P_2,..., characterize when there exists one action sequence a_t whose rewards track V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) for all T. Use paths 0^N,c_0,c_1 and multi-threshold paths as test cases, and look for a horizon-compatibility modulus that gives both lower and upper bounds.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Characterization","why_promising":"This targets the one quantifier regime where oracle collapse does not apply and where the same-path obstruction is genuinely instance-wise."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Delayed OCO/bandit results may not map cleanly because market-making feedback is full-information only after public threshold crossings and horizon censoring creates missing zero rewards; tracking all K grid arms may be too expensive without exploiting nesting.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature map","model translation"],"summary":"Replace fixed H truncation with adaptive skipping or tracking-capacity algorithms on the grid problem. Define skipped payoff mass or outstanding weighted feedback, use public prices to close all grid arms below P_s, and attempt a bound in terms of skipped value, total outstanding observations, or capacity C rather than beta(H). Compare with Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2025/2026 and delayed-weighted FTRL reductions.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It could produce a stronger and more usable sufficiency theorem that does not require knowing a tail schedule H_T and aligns with current delayed-learning literature."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"If the rare branch is fully coded, the example may only refute essential-sup tail conditions for process-aware learners; if it is not coded, regret may become nontrivial. All-horizon expected-value bounds need careful summability choices.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","conditional tails","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Finish a rare-history construction where positive-probability branches contain arbitrarily long payoff-relevant delays, keeping beta or gamma large through essential sup, while their ex-ante contribution is negligible and/or the branch code makes the optimal quote known. Choose p_n, L_n, and amplitudes so expected regret is o(T) but conditional tails never decay.","title":"Rare-History Tail Failure","why_promising":"This directly tests whether essential-sup tail assumptions are too strong even after payoff weighting, and would sharpen CE-006 into a reusable obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Always quoting 1/2 must be checked for every inter-spike horizon, not just spike endpoints; older blocks and strict-crossing nonattainment can add small but annoying terms.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample polishing","horizon accounting","Gamma stress test"],"summary":"Formalize the superexponential uniform-spike process: P_{S_n}=U_n with U_n uniform on [0,1], zeros otherwise, S_{n+1}/S_n -> infinity. Prove an anytime simple policy, likely always 1/2, has o(T) regret for all T, while gamma stays bounded away from zero and Delta_{S_n}(H)=Omega(S_n) for H=o(S_n). Determine whether Gamma_T(H) is also linear along spike horizons.","title":"Uniform-Spike Counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate predictable delayed reward mass from learning difficulty more cleanly than the beta-only sparse-vanishing-spike example."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Approachability controls vector averages under observed vector payoffs, while the market-making deficits involve future-censored rewards and possibly unknown candidate horizons; feedback availability may block a direct algorithm.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithmic upper bound","finite candidate reduction"],"summary":"Try to turn prefix compatibility from a lower-bound modulus into an algorithmic sufficient condition. For a finite set of candidate horizons or continuations, define vector deficits against each candidate and ask whether simultaneous Blackwell approachability gives one action rule with small regret to all candidates when kappa-like feasibility gaps vanish or are small.","title":"Simultaneous-Approachability Upper Bound","why_promising":"It offers a possible positive counterpart to the prefix-incompatibility lower bounds and uses new simultaneous approachability literature rather than only delayed-feedback tools."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may drift away from the stated pseudo-regret problem; raw beta may still be the wrong statistic even under pathwise regret, so the output may be only a taxonomy.","suggested_lenses":["regret-notion comparison","counterexample search","quantifier taxonomy"],"summary":"Systematically compare pseudo-regret sup_a E[reward(a)] with pathwise regret E[sup_a reward(a)-learner] and with endogenous/adaptive prices. Re-test oracle collapse, stochastic two-continuation mixtures, sparse spikes, and coded delays under each notion to identify whether any tail necessity statement can be salvaged.","title":"Alternative Regret Notions","why_promising":"The original iff may be false mainly because of the pseudo-regret and horizon/law-aware quantifiers; this direction could clarify exactly which nearby formulations remain mathematically meaningful."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_006_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2074:iterations/iter_006/idea_01/idea.md:6:Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stderr.log:978:iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:21:- **Action-space stress test:** If \(a=0\) is removed but actions arbitrarily close to \(0\) remain, the same pathology persists because \(\sup_a\) can approach the zero-action tail. If the action space is restricted to \([\delta,1]\), this particular counterexample no longer works.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stderr.log:1073:iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:58:The example exploits vanishing payoff scale. If the action space were restricted to \([\delta,1]\), this particular obstruction would disappear. If \(0\) is removed but arbitrarily small positive actions remain and \(\sup_a\) is still used, the same pathology persists as a supremum phenomenon.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stderr.log:1383:iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:17:- **Missing assumption:** The refutation depends on \(0\in[0,1]\) being an allowed action. If the action space were \([\delta,1]\), this exact obstruction disappears.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stderr.log:1391:iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:25:- **Positive action floor:** Restricting actions to \([\delta,1]\) kills the \(a=0\) witness. Then sparse spikes with heights \(1/s_n\to0\) eventually cannot be hit by any admissible action, so \(\beta\) may decay trivially.
