./logs/iter_007_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2415:iterations/iter_007/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_007_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_006_ideas.stderr.log:2193:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli","relevance":"Adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful terminology and contrast for delayed-fill feedback.","title":"Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"New delayed OCO model with tracking capacity and delayed-weighted reductions; relevant to adaptive truncation/skipping and outstanding-information bounds.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang","relevance":"Develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions, a possible framework for prefix-compatibility or simultaneous-horizon guarantees.","title":"Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider","relevance":"Recent simultaneous-regret work; useful analogy for one sequence of predictions/actions satisfying many downstream losses or horizons.","title":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jason D. Hartline","relevance":"Contains equal-revenue distribution and monopoly-pricing/revenue-curve background that may directly match the multi-threshold block LP.","title":"Mechanism Design and Approximation, Chapter 4: Bayesian Approximation","url":"https://jasonhartline.com/MDnA/MDnA-ch4.pdf","venue_year":"online textbook chapter"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The 1/e constant may fail under finite-support approximation, strict crossing, or transfer to concatenated paths; multi-horizon same-path versions must control intermediate quote rewards when the number of thresholds grows.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","lower-bound constants","mechanism-design analogy","strict-threshold cleanup"],"summary":"Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).","title":"Multi-Threshold Block Constant","why_promising":"This is a concrete, separately checkable extension of the accepted 1/4 lemma and may strengthen the lower-bound constant while connecting kappa to known posted-price geometry."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The prefix-local comparator v_u must be a valid lower bound on the global fixed-action comparator; same-prefix laws can fail for stochastic candidates; rare events and suffix compensation can otherwise create false positives.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's kappa transfer into a precise lemma. Work on finite grids and finite candidate set U first: condition on a common-prefix event E, assume the learner's prefix action marginals are identical for all u in U, define prefix payoffs phi_{u,t}, and include an explicit outside-surplus cap B_u plus probability weight Pr(E). Prove max_u regret_u >= |I| kappa_I(U)-B, or identify the missing hypotheses.","title":"Kappa Transfer Lemma","why_promising":"It would convert the current prefix-compatibility heuristic into an accepted reusable lower-bound tool, and every assumption can be criticized directly."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A full characterization may become tautological or computational rather than structural; the best fixed comparator couples all times through one quote, while the learner can vary quotes by time.","suggested_lenses":["offline characterization","finite examples","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Study the process-aware but horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix design problem. Given a known path P_1,P_2,..., characterize when there exists one action sequence a_t whose rewards track V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) for all T. Use paths 0^N,c_0,c_1 and multi-threshold paths as test cases, and look for a horizon-compatibility modulus that gives both lower and upper bounds.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Characterization","why_promising":"This targets the one quantifier regime where oracle collapse does not apply and where the same-path obstruction is genuinely instance-wise."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Delayed OCO/bandit results may not map cleanly because market-making feedback is full-information only after public threshold crossings and horizon censoring creates missing zero rewards; tracking all K grid arms may be too expensive without exploiting nesting.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature map","model translation"],"summary":"Replace fixed H truncation with adaptive skipping or tracking-capacity algorithms on the grid problem. Define skipped payoff mass or outstanding weighted feedback, use public prices to close all grid arms below P_s, and attempt a bound in terms of skipped value, total outstanding observations, or capacity C rather than beta(H). Compare with Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2025/2026 and delayed-weighted FTRL reductions.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It could produce a stronger and more usable sufficiency theorem that does not require knowing a tail schedule H_T and aligns with current delayed-learning literature."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"If the rare branch is fully coded, the example may only refute essential-sup tail conditions for process-aware learners; if it is not coded, regret may become nontrivial. All-horizon expected-value bounds need careful summability choices.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","conditional tails","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Finish a rare-history construction where positive-probability branches contain arbitrarily long payoff-relevant delays, keeping beta or gamma large through essential sup, while their ex-ante contribution is negligible and/or the branch code makes the optimal quote known. Choose p_n, L_n, and amplitudes so expected regret is o(T) but conditional tails never decay.","title":"Rare-History Tail Failure","why_promising":"This directly tests whether essential-sup tail assumptions are too strong even after payoff weighting, and would sharpen CE-006 into a reusable obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Always quoting 1/2 must be checked for every inter-spike horizon, not just spike endpoints; older blocks and strict-crossing nonattainment can add small but annoying terms.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample polishing","horizon accounting","Gamma stress test"],"summary":"Formalize the superexponential uniform-spike process: P_{S_n}=U_n with U_n uniform on [0,1], zeros otherwise, S_{n+1}/S_n -> infinity. Prove an anytime simple policy, likely always 1/2, has o(T) regret for all T, while gamma stays bounded away from zero and Delta_{S_n}(H)=Omega(S_n) for H=o(S_n). Determine whether Gamma_T(H) is also linear along spike horizons.","title":"Uniform-Spike Counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate predictable delayed reward mass from learning difficulty more cleanly than the beta-only sparse-vanishing-spike example."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Approachability controls vector averages under observed vector payoffs, while the market-making deficits involve future-censored rewards and possibly unknown candidate horizons; feedback availability may block a direct algorithm.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithmic upper bound","finite candidate reduction"],"summary":"Try to turn prefix compatibility from a lower-bound modulus into an algorithmic sufficient condition. For a finite set of candidate horizons or continuations, define vector deficits against each candidate and ask whether simultaneous Blackwell approachability gives one action rule with small regret to all candidates when kappa-like feasibility gaps vanish or are small.","title":"Simultaneous-Approachability Upper Bound","why_promising":"It offers a possible positive counterpart to the prefix-incompatibility lower bounds and uses new simultaneous approachability literature rather than only delayed-feedback tools."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may drift away from the stated pseudo-regret problem; raw beta may still be the wrong statistic even under pathwise regret, so the output may be only a taxonomy.","suggested_lenses":["regret-notion comparison","counterexample search","quantifier taxonomy"],"summary":"Systematically compare pseudo-regret sup_a E[reward(a)] with pathwise regret E[sup_a reward(a)-learner] and with endogenous/adaptive prices. Re-test oracle collapse, stochastic two-continuation mixtures, sparse spikes, and coded delays under each notion to identify whether any tail necessity statement can be salvaged.","title":"Alternative Regret Notions","why_promising":"The original iff may be false mainly because of the pseudo-regret and horizon/law-aware quantifiers; this direction could clarify exactly which nearby formulations remain mathematically meaningful."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
